# 03 — Multi-Viewport Layout (ViewportGrid) — Synced Camera + Per-Pane Layers

## 1. Concept

Split the viewport area into multiple panes. All panes share one camera — rotating in any pane rotates all panes simultaneously. Each pane controls which layers are visible, creating a scientific comparison view:

- Pane 1: shaded surface with scalar field coloring
- Pane 2: vector arrows only
- Pane 3: wireframe mesh + airbox boundary

## 2. Layout Presets

```typescript
type ViewportGridLayout =
  | "single"         // 1×1 — full viewport
  | "split-h"        // 1×2 — left + right
  | "split-v"        // 2×1 — top + bottom
  | "quad"           // 2×2 — four equal panes
  | "one-plus-two";  // large left + two stacked right
```

```
single:        split-h:       quad:          one-plus-two:
┌──────────┐   ┌─────┬─────┐  ┌─────┬─────┐  ┌────────┬────┐
│  shaded  │   │ vec │wire │  │shade│wire │  │ shaded │wire│
│  +vec    │   │field│frame│  ├─────┼─────┤  │ +vec   ├────┤
│  +field  │   │     │     │  │air  │flat │  │        │air │
└──────────┘   └─────┴─────┘  └─────┴─────┘  └────────┴────┘
```

## 3. Per-Pane Configuration

Each pane controls only **which layers are visible** and **display mode**. Camera is shared.

```typescript
interface ViewportPaneConfig {
  id: string;
  label: string;
  displayMode: DisplayMode;
  layers: PaneLayerVisibility;
}

interface PaneLayerVisibility {
  meshSurface: boolean;     // object meshes (cuboids or tets)
  scalarField: boolean;     // color overlay from field
  vectorGlyphs: boolean;    // arrow instances
  wireframe: boolean;       // edge overlay (= mesh view)
  airbox: boolean;          // airbox boundary
  axesGrid: boolean;        // coordinate axes + ground grid
  boundsBox: boolean;       // universe bounds wireframe
  selection: boolean;       // selection highlight
  clipPlane: boolean;       // clipping plane
}
```

### Default Presets by Layout

| Layout | Panes |
|---|---|
| `single` | `[{ shaded, all on }]` |
| `split-h` | `[{ shaded, mesh+field+vectors }, { wireframe, mesh+wireframe }]` |
| `split-v` | `[{ shaded, mesh+field }, { shaded, vectors+airbox }]` |
| `quad` | `[{ shaded, all }, { wireframe, mesh }, { shaded, airbox+bounds }, { flat-shaded, mesh }]` |
| `one-plus-two` | `[{ shaded, mesh+field+vectors }, { wireframe, mesh }, { shaded, airbox }]` |

## 4. Camera Synchronization

### Shared Camera Store

All panes read and write the same camera state:

```typescript
// In viewport-3d/store.ts
interface CameraState {
  position: [number, number, number];
  target: [number, number, number];
  up: [number, number, number];
  fov: number;
}

interface Viewport3DStoreState {
  camera: CameraState;
  setCamera: (patch: Partial<CameraState>) => void;
  // ...pane config, layout, etc.
}
```

### SyncedOrbitControls

Each `<Canvas>` mounts a `SyncedOrbitControls` that:
1. On user interaction → writes camera state to the shared store
2. On store change (from another pane) → applies camera state to this pane's controls

```tsx
function SyncedOrbitControls() {
  const controlsRef = useRef<OrbitControlsImpl>(null);
  const camera = useViewport3DStore((s) => s.camera);
  const setCamera = useViewport3DStore((s) => s.setCamera);
  const { invalidate } = useThree();
  const isInteracting = useRef(false);

  // Write: this pane's interaction → store
  const handleChange = useCallback(() => {
    if (!isInteracting.current) return;
    const c = controlsRef.current;
    if (!c) return;
    setCamera({
      position: c.object.position.toArray() as [number, number, number],
      target: c.target.toArray() as [number, number, number],
    });
  }, [setCamera]);

  // Read: store → this pane (triggered by another pane)
  useEffect(() => {
    if (isInteracting.current) return; // don't fight with our own writes
    const c = controlsRef.current;
    if (!c) return;
    c.object.position.fromArray(camera.position);
    c.target.fromArray(camera.target);
    c.update();
    invalidate();
  }, [camera, invalidate]);

  return (
    <OrbitControls
      ref={controlsRef}
      onChange={handleChange}
      onStart={() => { isInteracting.current = true; }}
      onEnd={() => { isInteracting.current = false; }}
      makeDefault
    />
  );
}
```

### Performance

- Zustand selectors → no React re-render, just `invalidate()`
- 4 canvases × 1 frame each per camera update = 4 frames
- All panes render in the same RAF tick
- Idle = 0 frames across all panes

## 5. CSS Grid

```css
.fm-viewport-grid {
  display: grid;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  gap: 1px;
  background: var(--fm-surface-0);
}

.fm-viewport-grid[data-layout="single"]       { grid-template: 1fr / 1fr; }
.fm-viewport-grid[data-layout="split-h"]      { grid-template: 1fr / 1fr 1fr; }
.fm-viewport-grid[data-layout="split-v"]      { grid-template: 1fr 1fr / 1fr; }
.fm-viewport-grid[data-layout="quad"]         { grid-template: 1fr 1fr / 1fr 1fr; }
.fm-viewport-grid[data-layout="one-plus-two"] { grid-template: 1fr 1fr / 2fr 1fr; }
.fm-viewport-grid[data-layout="one-plus-two"] > :first-child { grid-row: 1 / -1; }
```

## 6. ViewportGrid Component

```tsx
function ViewportGrid({ renderModel, kernel }: Props) {
  const layout = useViewport3DStore((s) => s.gridLayout);
  const panes = useViewport3DStore((s) => s.panes);
  const activePaneId = useViewport3DStore((s) => s.activePaneId);

  return (
    <div className="fm-viewport-grid" data-layout={layout}>
      {panes.map((pane) => (
        <ViewportPane
          key={pane.id}
          config={pane}
          renderModel={renderModel}
          isActive={pane.id === activePaneId}
          onActivate={() => useViewport3DStore.getState().setActivePane(pane.id)}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## 7. Per-Pane Toolbar

Small overlay in each pane corner:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│ ● Shaded ▾  │ □ ⊞ ▦ ↗ 🔲  │ [LABEL] │
│  mode dropdown  layer toggles   │
└─────────────────────────────────┘
```

- Display mode dropdown
- Layer toggle buttons (icons for mesh, wireframe, vectors, airbox, grid)
- Pane label (editable)

The toolbar is a DOM overlay positioned above the canvas, not inside Three.js.

## 8. Maximise / Restore

Double-click pane label → that pane CSS transitions to fill the grid (`grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 1 / -1`). Other panes get `display: none` — their canvases stop rendering. Double-click again to restore.

## 9. Active Pane

Clicking inside a pane sets it as active. Active pane:
- Shows subtle border (1px `var(--fm-accent)`)
- Receives keyboard shortcuts
- Is the target for ribbon commands (like "set display mode")

## 10. Keyboard Shortcuts

| Shortcut | Action |
|---|---|
| `Alt+1` | Single layout |
| `Alt+2` | Split horizontal |
| `Alt+3` | Split vertical |
| `Alt+4` | Quad |
| `Alt+5` | One + two |
| `Space` | Maximise/restore active pane |
| `Numpad 0` | Reset camera |
| `Z` | Toggle wireframe on active pane |
| `V` | Toggle vectors on active pane |
| `A` | Toggle airbox on active pane |
| `G` | Toggle axes grid on active pane |
| `Home` | Fit all objects |
